  "query_preview": "..(Thursday night through Tuesday)\nIssued at 535 AM EDT WED AUG 12 2026\n\nThe most current model data continues to suggest a pattern aloft \nthat favors the formation of storm clusters through Friday - most\nof which will impinge on the weather for eastern Kentucky. Right \nnow, SPC has continued a Marginal severe threat into Friday for \nthe bulk of our area. Meanwhile, WPC continues to highlight \nexcessive rain threats through Friday - though the concern is \nhigher earlier in the week. We continue to highlight this in \nmessaging and social media. Please keep monitoring this\ndeveloping, potentially impactful, weather situation. By the\nweekend things should be settling down, though \n\nThe previous long term discussion follows:\n\nWith a strong ridge across the Gulf of America and parts of the \nSouthern Mississippi Valley, and a strong upper level low in eastern \nCanada. The result will be strong NW to SE flow across the Ohio \nValley, including Kentucky on Thursday - a continuation from the \nshort term portion of the forecast. With an elongated surface \nboundary also set up across the Ohio Valley, this could continue the \npain train of MCS's passing southeast and through the region on \nThursday. In some promising news, the ridge does start to build \nnorthward on Friday, which will push the storm trajectory north a \nbit. However, could still impact the northeast portion of the state \nduring the day. The ridge will continue to build in further as we go \ninto Saturday, sustaining through Sunday, before it's pushed back \nsouthward during the day Monday as troughing increases along the \nAtlantic Coast, reintroducing the same NW to SE flow to the region \nagain through the rest of the forecast period. \n\nAs has been discussed over the past days, with each round of MCSs \nthat moves through, another round of heavy to torrential rainfall \nwill occur on increasingly saturated soils - so the flash flood \nthreat will remain a concern. CAMS have really not been handling \nthese MCSs well in the short term, both spatially or temporally. And \nif the CAMs aren't handling it well, the long-term models are also \nnot lending much confidence. Friday is also a bit interesting \nbecause the upper level forcing will be lessoning, but the surface \nboundary will finally drop southward into the state as a cold front, \nwhich could continue some convection across the region - though \nstill unclear if there will actually be an MCS or just more \nscattered to widespread convection. By Saturday, as ridging takes \nfurther hold and the surface boundary drops farther south of the \nstate, high pressure should generally be in control. This should \npersist into Sunday. The NBM does still have some very low end pops, \nmainly along the far eastern side of the state, but don't expect \nanything more at this point. \n\nWhen the pattern begins to set back up with SW flow on Monday into \nTuesday, WPC is also analyzing another cold front shifting southeast \nthrough the state as well, which, if it pans out, would bring yet \nanother increase in precipitation to the region. \n\nDespite the passage of cold fronts and numerous systems moving \nthrough, overall temperatures are forecast to rather steady-state, \nwith highs in the upper 80s and low 90s. Lows will be in the upper",
